  "nameStory": "",
  "description": "The Coral-winged Grasshopper (Pardalophora apiculata) is a medium-sized member of the Short-horned Grasshoppers family (Acrididae) found throughout New England. It is named for the distinctive coral or reddish coloring on its wings, which is visible when the insect flies.",
  "idTips": [
    {
      "label": "Coral-red Hind Wings",
      "description": "Bright coral or reddish-pink coloring on the hind wings is the most distinctive feature, visible when the grasshopper takes flight.",
      "isPrimary": true
    },
    {
      "label": "Mottled Brown Forewings",
      "description": "The front wings (tegmina) are typically mottled brown or tan, providing camouflage when at rest.",
      "isPrimary": false
    },
    {
      "label": "Medium Size and Build",
      "description": "This species is medium-sized for grasshoppers, with a robust body and moderate-length antennae typical of Acrididae.",
      "isPrimary": false
    }
  ],
  "habitat": "Coral-winged Grasshoppers inhabit open grasslands, meadows, and weedy fields throughout New England, preferring areas with mixed grasses and low vegetation.",
  "seasonality": {
    "spring": "",
    "summer": "",
    "fall": "",
    "winter": ""
  },
  "funFact": "The bright coral wing coloring serves as a warning signal to predators, which learn to associate the flash of color with an unpalatable or distasteful grasshopper.",
